  <title>9.4.1: Perfect Squares with Coefficients Other than 1</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02387</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.4.1: Perfect Squares with Coefficients Other than 1</md:title>
    <md:uuid>03bd5a5c-1deb-4e88-ab0e-abfd7828455b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Elena says, "\((x+3)^2\) can be expanded into \(x^2+6x+9\). Likewise, \((2x+3)^2\) can be expanded into \(4x^2+6x+9\)."</para>
    <para>Find an error in Elena's statement and correct the error. Show your reasoning.</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>\((2x+3)^2\) is not equivalent to \(4x^2+6x+9\).</para>
  <para>Applying the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">distributive property</term> to \((2x+3)(2x+3)\) gives \(4x^2+6x+6x+9\), which is \(4x^2+12x+9\).</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
</document>
